qs.append({"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the social base of the 19th century reform movements in India:\n1. Their social base was the newly emerging middle class and educated intelligentsia.\n2. This intelligentsia largely grew out of trade and industrial capital.\n3. Their roots mainly lay in government service and professions like law, education, journalism and medicine.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 3 only","1 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"The reform intelligentsia's base was the emerging middle class rooted in government service and professions (law, education, journalism, medicine), not trade or industry, which remained under British control."})

qs.append({"q":"What gave the 19th century socio-religious reform movements their ideological unity, as identified in the chapter?","o":["Rationalism, religious universalism and humanism","Strict revivalism and rejection of all Western ideas","Marxist class analysis","Exclusive focus on temple entry rights"],"a":0,"e":"Rationalism, religious universalism, and humanism together gave ideological unity to movements as diverse as the Brahmo Samaj and the Aligarh Movement."})

qs.append({"q":"The reform movements of the 19th century are broadly classified into which two categories?","o":["Reformist movements (e.g., Brahmo Samaj, Prarthana Samaj) and revivalist movements (e.g., Arya Samaj, Deoband)","Nationalist movements and communal movements","Rural movements and urban movements","Hindu movements and Muslim movements only"],"a":0,"e":"The chapter classifies reform movements into reformist (Brahmo Samaj, Prarthana Samaj, Aligarh Movement) and revivalist (Arya Samaj, Deoband movement) streams, differing in their reliance on tradition versus reason."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following was the earliest legislative measure banning the practice of sati, and to which regions did it originally apply?","o":["Regulation XVII of 1829, applicable first to the Bengal Presidency and extended to Madras and Bombay in 1830","Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act, 1856, applicable across all of British India","Age of Consent Act, 1891, applicable to Bengal only","Sarda Act, 1930, applicable to the whole of India"],"a":0,"e":"Sati was declared illegal by Regulation XVII of 1829 (Bengal Code), first applicable in Bengal Presidency, extended in modified form to Madras and Bombay Presidencies in 1830."})

qs.append({"q":"Raja Rammohan Roy, whose efforts led to the abolition of sati, described the practice as:","o":["\"Murder according to every shastra\"","\"A necessary evil sanctioned by scripture\"","\"An expression of religious devotion\"","\"A dying custom of no social consequence\""],"a":0,"e":"Raja Rammohan Roy strongly condemned sati, describing it as murder according to every shastra."})

qs.append({"q":"Which Bengal regulations first declared female infanticide illegal and equivalent to murder?","o":["Bengal Regulations of 1795 and 1804","Bengal Regulation of 1829","Age of Consent Act, 1891","Native Marriage Act, 1872"],"a":0,"e":"The Bengal Regulations of 1795 and 1804 declared female infanticide illegal, treating it as equivalent to murder."})

qs.append({"q":"The Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act, 1856, which legalised the remarriage of widows, was passed primarily due to the efforts of:","o":["Pandit Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","Raja Rammohan Roy","Jyotiba Phule","B.R. Ambedkar"],"a":0,"e":"Pandit Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar, principal of Sanskrit College Calcutta, was chiefly responsible for the Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act, 1856, citing Vedic texts to justify widow remarriage."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following correctly matches the reformer with their contribution to the widow remarriage movement?\n1. Karsondas Mulji - started Satya Prakash in Gujarati to advocate widow remarriage\n2. D.K. Karve - opened a widows' home in Poona and himself married a widow\n3. Vishnu Shastri Pandit - founded the Widow Remarriage Association\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["All three","Only two","Only one","None"],"a":0,"e":"All three pairs are correctly matched: Karsondas Mulji (Satya Prakash), D.K. Karve (widows' home in Poona, married a widow in 1893), and Vishnu Shastri Pandit (Widow Remarriage Association)."})

qs.append({"q":"The Native Marriage Act (or Civil Marriage Act) of 1872, aimed at controlling child marriage, had a limited impact because:","o":["It was not applicable to Hindus, Muslims and other recognised faiths","It was applied only to Christians and had no relevance to Indian society","It carried no penalty provisions at all","It applied only in the Bombay Presidency"],"a":0,"e":"The Native Marriage Act of 1872 excluded Hindus, Muslims and other recognised faiths from its ambit, sharply limiting its effect on child marriage."})

qs.append({"q":"The Age of Consent Act, 1891, which forbade the marriage of girls below the age of 12, was enacted largely due to the efforts of:","o":["B.M. Malabari","Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","Jyotiba Phule","Sri Narayana Guru"],"a":0,"e":"B.M. Malabari, a Parsi reformer, campaigned relentlessly for the Age of Consent Act, 1891."})

qs.append({"q":"The Sarda Act of 1930 raised the minimum age of marriage to:","o":["18 for boys and 14 for girls","21 for boys and 18 for girls","18 for boys and 15 for girls","15 for boys and 12 for girls"],"a":0,"e":"The Sarda Act, 1930 set the minimum marriage age at 18 for boys and 14 for girls; this was later revised by the Child Marriage Restraint (Amendment) Act, 1978, to 21 and 18 respectively."})

