qs.append({"q":"The struggle of the depressed classes led to which of the following constitutional/legislative provisions during the colonial period?","o":["Special representation for depressed classes in the Government of India Act, 1935","Universal adult franchise in the Government of India Act, 1935","Reservation in government jobs under the Charter Act, 1833","Abolition of untouchability under the Indian Councils Act, 1909"],"a":0,"e":"The struggle of the depressed classes resulted in special representation being provided for them in the Government of India Act, 1935."})

qs.append({"q":"Consider the following statements regarding caste-related organisational efforts by Dr Ambedkar:\n1. He organised the All India Scheduled Castes Federation.\n2. He established the Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha in 1924.\n3. He led the Mahad Satyagraha in 1927.\n4. He founded the All India Harijan Sangh in 1932.\nHow many of the above statements are correctly attributed to Ambedkar?","o":["Only three","All four","Only two","Only one"],"a":0,"e":"Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correctly attributed to Ambedkar. Statement 4 is incorrect — the All India Harijan Sangh (1932) was founded by Mahatma Gandhi, not Ambedkar."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following best describes Gandhi's stance on untouchability, as reflected in the chapter?","o":["He argued the Shastras did not sanction untouchability, and even if they did, they should be ignored since truth cannot be confined within a book","He argued that untouchability should be maintained as a religious duty","He believed caste and untouchability should be abolished only through violent revolution","He opposed any reform of caste practices, considering them purely a British concern"],"a":0,"e":"Gandhi held that the Shastras did not sanction untouchability, and even where scriptural sanction seemed to exist, such practices should be rejected on grounds of truth and humanism."})

qs.append({"q":"According to the chapter, the socio-cultural regeneration of 19th century India was:","o":["Occasioned by the colonial presence, but not created by it","Entirely a product of British colonial policy with no indigenous roots","A direct continuation of pre-colonial reform traditions without any Western influence","Solely the result of Christian missionary activity"],"a":0,"e":"The chapter emphasises that the reform movements were occasioned, not created, by the colonial presence — indigenous social conditions and intellectual currents were equally responsible."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following organisations/individuals are cited as early instruments of social reform before the national movement took over leadership in this sphere?","o":["The Social Conference, Servants of India Society, Christian missionaries, and individuals like Jyotiba Phule and B.M. Malabari","Only the Indian National Congress","Only the British colonial government","Only religious orthodox organisations opposing reform"],"a":0,"e":"Early social reform was driven by organisations such as the Social Conference, Servants of India Society, Christian missionaries, and reformist individuals like Jyotiba Phule, D.K. Karve, B.M. Malabari and Sri Narayana Guru, before the national movement assumed this leadership role in the twentieth century."})

qs.append({"q":"Which constitutional provision of free India explicitly addresses the abolition of untouchability?","o":["Untouchability is abolished and its practice in any form is forbidden, along with a Directive Principle promoting a just social order","Only a Directive Principle recommends abolishing untouchability, with no enforceable provision","Untouchability is addressed only through ordinary legislation, not the Constitution","The Constitution is silent on untouchability, leaving it to state laws"],"a":0,"e":"The Constitution of free India abolishes untouchability, forbids restrictions based on it (e.g., on wells, tanks, temples), and includes a Directive Principle on securing a just social order."})

